1. Basic Git Commands

Initialize a repository: git init

Stage all files: git add .

Commit with a message: git commit -m "Your commit message"

Push to remote: git push origin main

Pull latest changes: git pull

2. git fetch vs git pull

git fetch downloads the latest commits and branches from the remote repository to your local system without modifying your working files.

git pull fetches the remote changes and immediately merges them automatically into your current active local branch.

3. Repository Creation Request
As an AI, I do not have a local system filesystem, continuous access, or personal cloud credentials to create live web accounts and host public GitHub URLs. However, you can run these steps locally to create and push your own repository:

Bash
mkdir my-sample-repo
cd my-sample-repo
git init
echo "Hello World" > sample.txt
git add sample.txt
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/your-username/my-sample-repo.git
git push -u origin main
4. Fork vs Pull Request

A Fork is a complete server-side copy of someone else's GitHub repository created under your own user account.

A Pull Request (PR) is a formal request sent to the original repository's maintainers proposing to merge code changes from your branch/fork into their main codebase.

5. Git Branching Commands
To create a feature branch, commit a change, and merge it back into main:

Bash
# 1. Create and switch to the new branch
git checkout -b feature-test

# 2. Make changes and commit on feature-test
git add .
git commit -m "Add changes to feature-test"

# 3. Switch back to the main branch
git checkout main

# 4. Merge feature-test into main
git merge feature-test
